# THE STANDARD'S OWN TEST SUITE READS ITS SOURCES AS TEXT, LINE BY LINE.
#
# This repository is not only where the code lives - it is its own fixture.
# `unname_everywhere()` in `tests/test_update_shape_add.py` strips a copy-list
# entry by matching `    "<entry>",\n` as a WHOLE LINE, and other tests diff
# scaffolded output against `templates/assembly-root/` and
# `templates/family-root/` byte for byte. Git for Windows' installer default
# is `core.autocrlf=true`; a checkout of THIS repository made under it turns
# every `\n` in these sources into `\r\n`, the whole-line match above goes
# blind, and a colleague whose only sin was cloning on a stock Windows laptop
# watches the suite fail on lines nobody touched. CI never sees it: the Linux
# and macOS jobs check out LF by default and the Windows job pins
# `autocrlf=false`.
#
# `scripts/shape_materialize.py` also copies bytes - the two templates'
# `.gitattributes` among them - into every project and family holder it
# scaffolds, and `contracts/shape-pin.yaml` records a sha256 of each copy. A
# CRLF byte on disk is a different digest than the LF byte the pin records,
# which is exactly the failure the templates' OWN `.gitattributes` exists to
# prevent for a SCAFFOLDED project (#51, 2026-09-05). The standard's own root
# carried no such file, which is the gap this one closes: `* text=auto eol=lf`
# normalises text to LF in the object store on check-in and checks it out as
# LF on every platform, whatever `core.autocrlf` is set to - so nobody has to
# be told a git setting before cloning openRepoShape itself, and nobody who
# was never told is punished for it.
#
# NO PATH IS LISTED BY NAME, for the same reason the templates name none:
# every file this repository ships is text - Python, bash, YAML, Markdown, a
# workflow - so there is nothing for git's binary heuristic to misjudge, and
# `text=auto` leaves that detection where it belongs: with git.
#
# openRepoShape #63; the templates' own copy of this rule: #51 (2026-09-05).

* text=auto eol=lf
